  you use my link in the description or  scan my qr code you'll get a free  starter pack worth almost 40 dollars  we're talking free free champions at  once mizeracord tiger soul romero plus  10 magic xp brews 10 force xp brews and  10 spirit brews all this treasure will  be waiting for you here and remember  these rewards will only be available for  the next 30 days and for new players  only once you're in you can find me  in-game under the name 42 and if you're  fast you can join my clan it's that easy  just click the link in the description  and i'll see you in game at the turn of  the 19th century right here in england  there were well over 200 criminal  offences that were punishable by death  amongst them were such heinous crimes as  being in the company of gypsies for one  month  writing a threatening letter and brace  yourself stealing from a rabbit warren i  always knew those cuddly bastards were  hiding something put simply back then if  you broke the law in almost any way you  were basically [ __ ]  this absurdly strict criminal justice  system would eventually come to be known  as the bloody code but it was its very  harshness that catalysed a dramatic  shift in public sentiment towards how  criminals were treated i mean was it  really okay to have someone murdered for  writing a strongly worded letter  assuming they didn't continually use the  wrong spelling of their  probably not but of course such reckless  hate still needed to be punished  somehow  it was this need for an alternative to  capital punishment that saw the number  of state prisons being set up in england  rise dramatically in the early part of  the 19th century by and large  incarceration was seen as a positive  alternative to death but whilst prisons  were clearly a good place to house any  spare criminals you weren't planning on  hanging  not everyone was entirely convinced they  were the best way to encourage those  criminals to change their ways  you're probably familiar with the phrase  there's no rest for the wicked which is  usually used light-heartedly to mean  that work doesn't stop no matter how  tired or busy you may be but the phrase  originally comes from a passage in the  book of isaiah in the old testament of  the bible  that explains how sinners who end up in  the fiery pits of hell will experience  eternal torment and therefore never know  peace or rest again  and that whole concept raised an  interesting question if never-ending  torture was god's way of punishing  evildoers down in hell was prison really  the best solution for dealing with them  up here on earth because in prison which  was basically just a giant building full  of tiny rooms where nobody ever did  anything  there was plenty of rest for the wicked  in the early 19th century this little  discrepancy between the will of god and  the approach taken in england's prisons  came to the attention of a young british  engineer by the name of william cubit  he'd recently visited barry  saint-edmonds jail and been outraged to  witness widespread idleness amongst the  inmates there so outraged in fact that  he decided he was going to do something  about it coming from a family of millers  cubit reasoned that the best thing for  these lazy criminals was to give them a  taste of what genuine hard work looked  like but that was easier said than done  after all there wasn't exactly an  abundance of useful jobs to choose from  nor was there any space for physical  activity cubit needed to come up with a  way of putting large numbers of inmates  to work without taking up too much  precious real estate his solution as i'm  sure you've guessed by now was the  treadmill admittedly cubit's original  design was fairly different to the  treadmills that can be found in homes  and gyms all over the world today it was  basically just a giant rotating wheel  complete with large flat spokes that a  prisoner could climb a bit like an  endless staircase as the wheel rotated  and here's where the mill part of the  name will finally start to make sense  you see the largest treadmills could  accommodate as many as 40 prisoners at  once  and cubit realized he could harness the  energy they generated to do useful  things some prisons use this energy to  pump water or circulate air whilst  others use the treadmills like giant  human powered mills to grind corn in  some cases all the energy was harnessed  to do  absolutely nothing at all with prisoners  working those treadmills jokingly  claiming to be grinding the wind  at this point you might be wondering  what all the fuss is about can a spot of  exercise on one of cubits treadmills  really have been that bad after all  these days people pay good money to  grind the wind on the running machines  and steppers at their local gyms  but we aren't talking about a leisurely  5k followed by a protein shake and half  an hour in the sauna here  prisoners over the age of 16 in england  who were sentenced to hard labor were  forced to work the treadmill seven days  a week for between six and ten hours a  day with minimal breaks during that time  inmates will climb somewhere north of 4  000 vertical meters the equivalent of  scaling mount albert the tallest  mountain in the rockies every single day  now that's pretty insane but the reality  was actually even worse if for some  reason you did climb mount albert every  day  you would at least be rewarded with  lungfuls of crisp mountain air  incredible views and that exquisite  feeling of superiority over all your  friends every time you posted a summit  picture on instagram but prisoners who  were forced to work the treadmill  had no such return on their physical  investment  the machines were carefully designed to  make the whole experience as  mind-numbing as possible most were built  inside enclosed stalls meaning that far  from having a lovely view  prisoners had no choice but to stare at  a blank featureless wall hour after hour  as they climbed and many prisons added  partitions to prevent inmates from  talking or even seeing each other to top  it all off prison rations were woefully  inadequate for such a relentless  schedule of physical exertion meaning  prisoners would slowly grow weak and  sickly as their sentences dragged on  which only served to make each day  harder than the last  this combination of brutal physical  hardship and mind-numbing boredom  brought about by almost total sensory  deprivation made the treadmill not just  an incredibly grueling form of  punishment but a genuinely cruel one too  hardened criminals frequently collapsed  midway through a shift particularly in  the summer months  and there are reports of formerly  sanguine individuals becoming more or  less unhinged by week after week of  monotonous stair climbing in a few cases  the ordeal is even forced to have proven  fatal probably the most famous person  ever to have been made to walk the  treadmill is none other than legendary  author and playwright oscar wilde who in  1895 was imprisoned for the heinous  crime of being a homosexual  as part of his sentence wilde was forced  to suffer on the treadmill at  pentonville prison in london for a  period of three months and he was left  so exhausted and weakened by this  experience he collapsed one day in the  prison chapel it was a nasty fall and  wilde came away from it with a ruptured  eardrum an injury that some doctors  believe contributed to his death which  came just two years after he was  released from prison at the age of 46.  despite its incredible cruelty for a  long time the treadmill was seen as a  great success  largely thanks to a deluge of glowing  five-star reviews from prison wardens  who invariably reported less trouble  amongst inmates after a treadmill was  installed not that that's exactly a big  surprise in order to make trouble you  have to actually move something that  prisoners who used a treadmill for eight  hours a day  were understandably disinclined to do by  the end of the 19th century there was  almost 40 treadmills in use in prisons  around britain with several institutions  in the u.s and beyond also adopting the  technology  but over the years it seems the powers  that be slowly came to the shocking  realization that prisoners are actually  human beings too  and that both physically and mentally  torturing them was well all things  considered  probably a bit unnecessary the treadmill  was finally banned in england in 1902  and that should probably have been that  but some 60 years later an enterprising  american chap by the name of william  storm saw a new use for this outlawed  torture of prisoners  home fitness  treadmill-like devices were already  being used by doctors to test patient  heart rates at the time but it was storm  who first saw a gap in the market for a  running machine that could be used at  home and what a colossal gap it turned  out to be as of today the treadmill is  the world's most popular piece of gym  equipment by quite a distance  with global sales topping 3 billion  dollars  in 2020  of course it's hard to miss the irony  here  these days not only do people willingly  jump on these literal torture devices  they usually pay large sums of money for  the privilege but then as is often the  case context is everything in the hands  of a surgeon in an operating theatre a  scalpel is a life-saving tool  wielded by a thug in a street fight it's  a lethal weapon the same can be said of  the treadmill in a prison setting where  people were forced to use it for up to  10 hours a day it was a brutal torture  device  but when you can push the big red stop  button whenever you feel like it  it's just one of the perks of a hundred  pound a month gym membership  thanks for watching
 

